", "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null, "action": null, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "call-7ab4fee8-998c-4fb0-84bd-808beb5c6fd6", "tool_call": {"id": "call-7ab4fee8-998c-4fb0-84bd-808beb5c6fd6", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{"uncertainties":"[\"Hardware target (A100 vs H100 vs consumer GPU) is unspecified and materially changes feasibility and whether FP8/70B are in scope.\", \"Whether 70B and FP8 should be included in the core design or only as extensions.\", \"Depth of accuracy evaluation (perplexity only vs. perplexity + downstream tasks) and which datasets/tasks.\", \"Exact sequence-length and batch-size sweep needed to characterize the prefill/decode tradeoff.\", \"Runtime/hardware budget for the actual experiment this design would guide is not given in the brief.\", \"Exact QuaRot version/implementation pin for reproducibility.\"","stage":"plan","evidence":"Workspace listing (ls -la /workspace and find -maxdepth 3) shows only task.json. task.json read confirms the question, the \"design-only / do not claim an experiment was run\" context, and documents.track.md = \"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\" Environment probe: python3 --version = 3.14.4; nproc = 224; free = 2.0Ti total; nvidia-smi fails (no GPU, matches gpu_enabled:false); torch and transformers not installed; network disabled. Therefore no QuaRot code, no LLaMa-2 checkpoints, no benchmark artifacts exist locally; the design is grounded in the public QuaRot paper (Ashkboos et al., NeurIPS 2024) and standard quantization/LLM-benchmarking practice.","assumptions":"[\"Workspace is design-only: no QuaRot code, LLaMa-2 checkpoints, or benchmark data present locally; all specifics come from the public QuaRot paper and standard practice.\", \"QuaRot 'end-to-end' = 4-bit weights + 4-bit dynamic KV-cache + 4-bit dynamic activations (per paper), to be confirmed.\", \"Target hardware assumed single A100-80GB (flexible); FP8 and 70B treated as optional extensions.\", \"7B/13B are core model sizes; 70B optional/secondary.\", \"Primary accuracy metric = perplexity on a fixed eval split; downstream task accuracy = optional secondary.\", \"The real experiment's runtime budget is unspecified; core matrix designed to fit a single A100-80GB in hours-to-a-day.\"","summary":"Report workspace findings and propose experiment design outline","answer":"## 1. Repository / input inspection\n\n**The workspace is effectively empty — we are working purely from the project brief and general knowledge.**\n\n- `/workspace` contains exactly ONE file: `task.json`. No reference papers, no QuaRot code, no LLaMa-2 checkpoints, no configs, no benchmark scripts, no documentation.\n- `task.json` contents: the question (\"How does QuaRot's end-to-end 4-bit quantization affect the prefill and decoding performance of LLaMa-2 models?\"), the context (\"Design an experiment that answers the question... Do not claim that an experiment was run\"), and a `documents.track.md` note: **\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\"**\n- Environment confirmed: Python 3.14.4, 224 CPU cores, ~2 TB RAM, **no GPU** (nvidia-smi fails, `gpu_enabled: false`), **no torch, no transformers**, network disabled. So this is a paper design, not an executed run.\n\nConclusion: I will not invent repository facts. Everything in the design will be grounded in the public QuaRot paper (Ashkboos et al., NeurIPS 2024) and standard LLaMa-2 / quantization-benchmarking practice, with source-supported details flagged as assumptions where the brief is silent.\n\n## 2. Proposed approach outline\n\n**(a) Key performance dimensions — prefill vs. decoding.** I'll treat these as two distinct regimes and measure both latency and memory, plus accuracy:\n- **Prefill** (long prompt, compute-bound, one forward pass over all tokens): prefill latency (ms), prefill throughput (tokens/s), peak GPU memory during prefill.\n- **Decoding** (single-token autoregressive, memory-bandwidth-bound): time-per-output-token (TPOT), decode throughput (tokens/s), steady-state decode memory.\n- **Memory footprint** (both): model weight size (GB) per precision, and KV-cache size per sequence length (critical — QuaRot's dynamic activation quantization directly targets KV-cache memory).\n- **Accuracy degradation**: perplexity on fixed eval set(s) and (optional) downstream task accuracy, compared to the FP16 (and where relevant FP8) reference.\n- **End-to-end wall-clock** for a full prompt→completion to tie the two regimes together.\n\n**(b) LLaMa-2 sizes and why.** Primary: **7B** and **13B** (single-GPU, well within one A100-80GB/H100, matches the paper's headline single-GPU results and are the sizes a realistic experiment can actually run). **70B** treated as an optional/secondary case (multi-GPU or offload) because it changes the memory story most dramatically for KV-cache/weights but is expensive; I'll include it as a clearly-marked extension, not a core condition.\n\n**(c) Isolating QuaRot from baselines.** Controlled comparison, everything else held constant (same checkpoints, same hardware, same prompt/sequence-length sweep, same batch size, same runtime/eval harness, same warmup):\n- **QuaRot 4-bit end-to-end** (the treatment): 4-bit weights + 4-bit dynamic KV-cache + 4-bit dynamic activations.\n- **FP16** — accuracy and speed reference (upper bound on quality, lower bound on compression).\n- **8-bit** (e.g., SmoothQuant / generic W8A8) — intermediate compression baseline.\n- **Other 4-bit weight-only** (e.g., GPTQ/AWQ-style 4-bit, no activation/KV quantization) — this is the key control to attribute QuaRot's gains specifically to its *end-to-end* (activation + KV-cache) scheme rather than just 4-bit weights.\n- Optionally **FP8** as a modern high-quality reference if hardware supports it.\nThis lets me separate \"benefit of 4-bit weights\" from \"additional benefit of QuaRot's activation/KV end-to-end scheme.\"\n\n**(d) Structure of the design document.**\n1. **Objective & research question** (restated, testable form).\n2. **Background** (QuaRot's mechanism: Hadamard rotations, dynamic quantization of activations/KV, why prefill vs decode differ).\n3. **Hypotheses** (e.g., QuaRot ≈ FP16 accuracy at ~4× weight compression; decode speedup from smaller weights + KV; prefill throughput tradeoff from dequant/rotation overhead).\n4. **Variables** — independent (precision/scheme, model size, sequence length, batch size), dependent (the metrics in (a)), controlled (hardware, checkpoints, harness, warmup, seed).\n5. **Experimental conditions / matrix** (schemes × sizes × sequence lengths × batch sizes).\n6. **Hardware & software setup** (target GPU, framework, QuaRot version, eval harness, warmup protocol).\n7. **Datasets / prompts & eval protocol** (perplexity corpus, optional downstream tasks, prompt/decode lengths).\n8. **Measurement protocol** (timing methodology, warmup iterations, iteration counts, memory measurement method).\n9. **Controls & validity** (the baseline isolation in (c), confound control, statistical handling — multiple runs/mean±std).\n10. **Analysis plan** (primary comparisons, effect sizes, expected results, how to read prefill vs decode tradeoffs, ablations).\n11. **Limitations & assumptions.**\n12. **Reproducibility / feasibility notes.**\n\n## 3. Consequential assumptions & uncertainties (brief is silent on all of these)\n\nEach is **flexible** unless noted. These materially change the design, so I'm flagging them for your decision:\n\n1. **Model sizes** — 7B/13B core, 70B optional. *Flexible.* (70B is multi-GPU and costly on a design-only budget.)\n2. **Hardware target** — assumed single **A100-80GB** (paper-aligned, can host 7B/13B at all precisions incl. FP16). H100 changes absolute numbers and enables FP8. *Flexible.* This is the single biggest variable for feasibility and for whether FP8/70B are in scope.\n3. **Definition of \"end-to-end\" for QuaRot** — I'll assume QuaRot's full scheme = **4-bit weights + 4-bit dynamic KV-cache + 4-bit dynamic activations**, per the paper. Whether the experiment *enables* KV-cache and activation quantization (vs. weights-only 4-bit) is exactly what the control in (c) isolates. *Flexible / to confirm.*\n4. **Accuracy metrics** — I'll default to **perplexity on a fixed eval split (e.g., WikiText-2/PTB/C4 subset)** as primary, with **downstream task accuracy (e.g., a small set like ARC / HellaSwag / a math/IFEval subset)** as secondary. Both is most defensible but downstream adds cost. *Flexible.*\n5. **Sequence-length / batch sweep** — I'll propose a small sweep (e.g., prefill lengths 512/2048/4096; decode up to 512 tokens; batch 1 and a small batch) to characterize the prefill/decode tradeoff. *Flexible.*\n6. **Runtime/hardware budget for the *actual* experiment this design guides** — not specified. I'll design so the **core matrix is runnable on a single A100-80GB in a few hours to a day**, with 70B/FP8/large-batch as clearly-marked optional extensions. *Flexible / to confirm.*\n7. **Baseline set** — FP16 + 8-bit + weight-only 4-bit are my defaults; adding FP8 or more 4-bit methods is optional. *Flexible.*\n8. **QuaRot version / implementation** — I'll assume the official QuaRot repo (NeurIPS 2024). Specific version pinning is a reproducibility detail I'll list as an assumption. *Flexible.*\n\n**Hard constraints (from the brief/environment, not assumptions):** design-only (no execution, no claimed results); no GPU in this sandbox (so all performance claims are design targets, not measurements); network disabled; do not claim an experiment was run.\n\n**Biggest uncertainties for you to resolve:** (1) hardware target, (2) whether to include 70B and FP8, (3) how deep the accuracy evaluation should go (perplexity only vs. + downstream), and (4) the runtime budget for the real experiment.\n\nI have NOT written the full design yet — awaiting your review of this outline before committing."}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-26ea208cb7ef4b23a6426b37279a7038", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "critic_result": null, "summary": null, "kind": "ActionEvent"}
